0:{"rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[[["$","$L2","1.7",{"topic":{"id":"1.7","title":"Comparison of IPC/CrPC/Evidence Act with BNS/BNSS/BSA","minutes":18,"summary":"Use a functional crosswalk—not a blind find-and-replace—to understand India’s criminal-law transition.","sections":[{"title":"Three different jobs","body":["The IPC and now the BNS define general offences and punishments. The CrPC and now the BNSS provide criminal procedure. The Indian Evidence Act and now the BSA regulate proof in proceedings. The IT Act continues as a special law; it was not replaced by the BNS. A case can require all four: a BNS offence, BNSS procedure, BSA admissibility, and an IT Act provision.","The new laws generally commenced on 1 July 2024. BNSS section 534 and BNS section 358 contain savings and transitional provisions. The date of the conduct, investigation, proceeding, and the relevant legal action can matter. “Old case = always old procedure” and “new filing = always new substantive law” are both unsafe shortcuts."]},{"title":"Important functional mappings","body":["Common offence mappings include IPC 419 to BNS 319(2), IPC 420 to BNS 318(4), IPC 465 to BNS 336(2), IPC 468 to BNS 336(3), and using a forged document under IPC 471 to BNS 340(2). These are study comparisons, not claims that every element and punishment is identical. BNS section 318 expressly retains the old section 420 reference in its marginal context, but always quote the operative provision.","For procedure, CrPC 91 corresponds broadly to BNSS 94, CrPC 154 to BNSS 173, CrPC 161 to BNSS 180, CrPC 164 to BNSS 183, and CrPC 165 to BNSS 185. Electronic FIR and audiovisual search provisions add procedural detail. Evidence Act 65A and 65B correspond broadly to BSA 62 and 63. BSA 63 adds an express certificate format and two signatories; the old form cannot simply be relabelled."]},{"title":"A safe comparison method","body":["First identify the legal question. Next read the old provision, the new provision, and any amendment or saving clause. Compare elements, scope, exceptions, procedure, and punishment separately. Finally check case law interpreting the wording. Similar chapter placement does not establish equivalence. Some provisions are reorganized, expanded, omitted, or newly framed.","For a digital investigation, the most important practical change is not a new section number. It is the need to align collection, device particulars, hash documentation, and certification under the applicable evidence regime. The comparison tool in this course gives a quick orientation; the official bare Acts remain the authority for a filing or examination answer."]}],"practice":{"title":"Repair the old charge template","scenario":"A post-2024 complaint template alleges IPC 420 and attaches a certificate headed “Section 65B”.","steps":["Confirm the date and nature of the alleged conduct and applicable transition rules.","Compare the ingredients of IPC 420 with BNS 318(4).","Review the electronic output under BSA 63 and the current Schedule.","Record differences rather than merely replacing the headings."],"debrief":"A defensible update checks substantive ingredients and evidentiary conditions. A certificate with the wrong statutory basis can create avoidable disputes."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["bns","bnss","bsa","ipc","crpc","iea"],"sources":["bns","bnss","bsa","ipc","crpc","iea"],"labId":"certificate","unit":1,"objectives":["Explain three different jobs and apply it to an investigation.","Explain important functional mappings and apply it to an investigation.","Explain a safe comparison method and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-1.7","prompt":"Which pairing correctly describes proof of electronic records?","options":["IEA 65B → BSA 63","IPC 420 → BNSS 94","CrPC 91 → BNS 318","IT Act 66D → BSA 63"],"answer":0,"explanation":"BSA 63 broadly succeeds Evidence Act 65B, with important changes including the scheduled certificate.","unit":1}}}]],["$L3"],"$L4"]}],"isPartial":false,"staleTime":300,"varyParams":null,"buildId":"mMiCr23A_-Cpk0hZkfT7X"}
